//! Slack, for agent channels: every agent on Slack is its own Slack app,
//! generated by Zupflow in the customer's team from a manifest.
//!
//! - [`provision::SlackProvisioner`][], the configuration-token
//!   [`AppProvisioner`](zupflow_core::channels::provision::AppProvisioner):
//!   checks and rotates a linked team's configuration token, makes, changes
//!   and deletes agents' apps, and installs one with the consent the person
//!   gives on Slack's page.
//! - [`events::SlackChannelProvider`][], the
//!   [`ChannelProvider`](zupflow_core::channels::provider::ChannelProvider)
//!   for Slack's Events API: verifies a delivery's signature with the app's
//!   own signing secret, and reads its events into Zupflow's terms.
//! - [`text`][], Slack's markup turned into stored text by one deterministic
//!   function, with fixtures.
//! - [`send::SlackClient`][], what an installed app does with its bot token:
//!   send, edit and delete its messages, react, open DMs, read people,
//!   conversations and history, show that it's working, and settle a send
//!   whose answer was lost.
//! - [`manifest`][], a generated app's manifest and its digest.
//!
//! Every call goes through the
//! [`OutboundHttp`](zupflow_core::outbound::OutboundHttp) it's given (the
//! safe outbound path in a deployment), never a client of its own. Statuses
//! are classified in [`api`], Slack's own words are never passed on, and
//! nothing here retries or sleeps: a rate limit is an error carrying Slack's
//! `Retry-After`, for a job to wait out.

